Medical Certificate (Short-term Illness)
General Practice Other
Acute self-limiting illness, Mild functional impairment, Verification of symptoms, Documentation of incapacity dates

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, highly contagious viral gastroenteritis requiring physical verification of symptoms for a mandatory, time-critical occupational medical certificate.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a food-handling professional experiencing acute, severe vomiting and diarrhea, preventing them from attending a highly critical shift tonight. The patient's employer strictly mandates a physical medical assessment and certificate prior to the missed shift to authorize the absence and ensure public health compliance. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to assess for severe dehydration, clinically verify the highly infectious symptoms to prevent a public health outbreak at a commercial food facility, and issue the necessary legal documentation. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would violate occupational health laws and risk the patient attempting to work while infectious out of fear of termination.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I've been vomiting constantly since this morning, I have severe diarrhea, and my boss says I'm fired if I don't get a doctor's note tonight before my shift starts."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an acute onset of a self-limiting infectious illness, specifically uncomplicated viral gastroenteritis. The symptoms began 10 hours ago following exposure to a sick family member. The patient experiences mild to moderate functional impairment, completely preventing safe or effective attendance at work. There is a brief expected duration of symptoms typically resolving within 3 to 7 days. The patient reports a low-grade fever, severe nausea, and gastrointestinal upset. There is a complete absence of severe red flag symptoms indicating a serious underlying pathology requiring hospital admission, such as hematemesis or severe, localized right lower quadrant pain.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted to the vicinity of the bathroom due to frequent diarrhea.
Social/Safety Concerns: High occupational stress; immense pressure to attend work despite being highly infectious in a food-service environment.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Appearing fatigued, pale, holding an emesis bag, but conversant and alert.
Physical Exam: Abdominal: Soft, generalized mild discomfort, hyperactive bowel sounds (borborygmi) auscultated in all four quadrants. No focal tenderness, no guarding, no rebound. Cardiovascular: Capillary refill is brisk (<2 seconds), moist mucous membranes, indicating no severe physiological dehydration despite the fluid loss. The presentation requires a direct clinical assessment to verify the reported symptoms, leading to the documentation of the specific dates of advised incapacity. Expected investigation results include normal vital signs and a benign physical examination confirming a self-limiting illness without the need for complex diagnostic testing.
Environmental Observations: Bathroom shows evidence of recent frequent use; patient has oral rehydration salts prepared.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Medical Certificate (Short-term Illness) (Acute Viral Gastroenteritis) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of focal pain migrating to the right lower quadrant, absence of rebound tenderness, and the prominent, immediate presence of massive watery diarrhea which is atypical for early appendicitis.

Bacterial Food Poisoning (e.g., Salmonella) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While possible, the management remains identical (supportive hydration) in the absence of bloody diarrhea or severe systemic toxicity.

Severe Dehydration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Maintained blood pressure, normal heart rate, and moist mucous membranes, indicating the patient is successfully keeping up with oral fluid replacement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High public health risk if working)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient exhibited acute, highly infectious gastrointestinal symptoms requiring immediate occupational exclusion, particularly given their role in food handling. Telephone triage correctly identified the public health imperative to keep the patient isolated. Urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically required to physically rule out a surgical abdomen (e.g., appendicitis), confirm clinical hydration status, and issue the mandatory medical certificate to legally enforce the occupational exclusion and alleviate the severe psychological stress of threatened termination. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period would have likely resulted in the patient attending their commercial food shift, initiating a mass public health transmission event.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough abdominal examination verifying the benign, self-limiting nature of the illness. Provided reassurance and advice on symptomatic management including strict hydration and simple analgesia. Issued a formal Medical Certificate documenting the specific dates of advised incapacity (exclusion from work for 48 hours post-resolution of the last episode of vomiting/diarrhea). Administered oral Ondansetron 4mg to settle the nausea.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain adequate hydration without triggering further vomiting, strictly adhere to the "sip, don't gulp" method. Place a single shot glass next to your water jug. Only drink exactly one shot glass of oral rehydration fluid every 10 minutes. By physically restricting the volume you can drink at one time, you prevent rapid gastric distension, successfully bypassing the mechanical trigger for vomiting and reinforcing successful fluid retention.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only required if symptoms persist beyond 5 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department if you begin vomiting bright red blood, if you pass dark black or bloody stools, or if you develop severe, sudden, doubling-over abdominal pain that does not go away after passing a bowel motion.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The vomiting should cease within 24 hours, followed by a few days of loose stools. The illness is highly contagious, and strict hand hygiene is mandatory.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you become so dizzy that you cannot stand up, or if you completely stop passing urine for more than 12 hours.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: You have a viral stomach bug. There are no antibiotics that will cure a virus; the only treatment is preventing dehydration while your immune system clears it. You are legally excluded from food handling until 48 hours after your symptoms stop completely.
Actionable Steps: Give the medical certificate to your employer immediately. Use the shot-glass method for drinking. Wash your hands thoroughly with soap and water; alcohol sanitizer does not kill stomach viruses effectively.

